.TE
.PP
Shortcuts use the key's position on the Latin layout, so they work the same
while another layout is active. A client that inhibits shortcuts (a remote
desktop or virtual machine window) receives all of them except Super+L and
the virtual terminal keys.
.PP
In the window picker Tab and the arrow keys move, releasing Alt selects and
Esc cancels. In Task View the arrow keys move, Enter selects, Delete closes
the window and Esc leaves. After Move or Size from the window menu the
arrow keys move or resize the window (with Ctrl: by single pixels) until
Enter or Esc; a window being moved also follows the pointer until a click.
.SH POINTER, TOUCH AND GESTURES
Drag a title bar to move a window; drag it to a screen edge to snap it (top:
maximize; sides: halves; the outer quarter of a side: that corner), with a
preview of the result. Double-click a title bar to maximize; right-click it
for the window menu, which also sends the window to another monitor or
desktop. Drag the border or a corner to resize. Super+left drag
moves and Super+right drag resizes from anywhere on a window.
.PP
Resting the pointer on the maximize button opens the snap layouts: two
halves, a half beside two quarters, and four quarters. Clicking a zone puts
the window there.
.PP
On a touch screen a finger does what the pointer does on title bars,
buttons, the window picker and Task View. On a touchpad, three or four
fingers sideways go to the next or previous desktop, up opens Task View and
down shows the desktop (\fB[touchpad] gestures\fR). Pinch and hold gestures
are passed to applications. Drawing tablets and pens are supported with
pressure, tilt and their buttons.
.SH CONFIGURATION
TOML files, later ones overriding earlier ones key by key:
.PP
.nf
/usr/share/boswas/compositor/compositor.toml   shipped defaults
/etc/boswas/compositor.toml                    the administrator
~/.config/boswas/compositor.toml               the user (written by Settings)
~/.config/boswas/appearance.toml               written by boswas\-preset
.fi
.PP
A file that does not parse, or that contains an unknown key, is reported and
ignored as a whole. \fIappearance.toml\fR may contain the [appearance] table
only. \fBboswas\-compositor \-\-send reload\-config\fR applies changes to a
running session.
.TP
.B [keyboard]
\fBlayout\fR, \fBvariant\fR, \fBmodel\fR, \fBoptions\fR (XKB names, for
example layout "us,in"); \fBrepeat_delay\fR (milliseconds),
\fBrepeat_rate\fR (per second); \fBnumlock\fR (on when the session starts).
.TP
.B [pointer]
Mice and trackballs: \fBnatural_scroll\fR, \fBaccel_speed\fR (\-1.0 to 1.0),
\fBaccel_profile\fR ("adaptive" or "flat"), \fBleft_handed\fR,
\fBcursor_theme\fR, \fBcursor_size\fR.
.TP
.B [touchpad]
\fBtap_to_click\fR, \fBnatural_scroll\fR, \fBaccel_speed\fR,
\fBdisable_while_typing\fR, \fBgestures\fR.
.TP
.B [output]
\fBscale\fR: 1.0, 1.25, 1.5, 1.75 or 2.0, for every output that has no
setting of its own.
.TP
.BI [outputs. NAME ]
One monitor, by connector name (\fBeDP\-1\fR, \fBHDMI\-A\-1\fR; see
\fB\-\-send outputs\fR): \fBenabled\fR, \fBmode\fR ("1920x1080" or
"1920x1080@60", one of the modes the monitor offers), \fBscale\fR,
\fBposition\fR ([x, y] of its top-left corner in logical pixels) and
\fBrotation\fR ("normal", "90", "180" or "270"). Without a position,
monitors are placed side by side from left to right. The last enabled
monitor is never turned off.
.TP
.B [night_light]
\fBenabled\fR; \fBtemperature\fR (2500 to 6500 kelvin); \fBscheduled\fR with
\fBfrom\fR and \fBto\fR ("HH:MM", local time) turns it on by the clock.
Needs a graphics driver with gamma tables.
.TP
.B [power]
\fBscreen_off_seconds\fR: time without input before the screens turn off (0:
never). Any input turns them on again.
.TP
.B [lock]
\fBidle_seconds\fR: time without input before the session locks (0: never,
if the policy allows it). \fBcommand\fR: the lock screen program.
.TP
.B [shell]
\fBcommand\fR: the shell program.
.TP
.B [input_method]
\fBcommand\fR: the input method program, for example ["fcitx5"]; empty for
none.
.PP
.RS
The three \fBcommand\fR keys are read from the first two files only: a
user's file cannot name these programs. The shell and the lock screen are
started as trusted clients. The input method is offered the input-method
and virtual-keyboard protocols only (it sees what is typed and types
itself) and none of the shell's other privileges.
.RE
.TP
.B [xwayland]
\fBenabled\fR: run an X server for X11 applications (default: true). A
user can turn it off; when the first two files turn it off, a user's file
does not turn it back on.
.TP
.B [apps]
\fBterminal\fR (Ctrl+Alt+T), \fBfiles\fR (Super+E), \fBsettings\fR
(Super+I) and \fBtask_manager\fR (Ctrl+Shift+Esc), each a program with its
arguments.
.TP
.B [appearance]
\fBscheme\fR ("dark" or "light"), \fBreduced_motion\fR (no animations),
\fBwallpaper\fR (a PNG or JPEG file shown by the shell instead of the
default background).
.TP
.B [policy]
Read from the first two files only; ignored in a user's file.
\fBlock_idle_max_seconds\fR: the longest lock timeout a user may choose (0:
no limit). \fBprivileged_protocols\fR: "shell" (default) or "all" (see
SECURITY).
.SH X11 APPLICATIONS
With \fB[xwayland] enabled\fR the compositor starts
.BR Xwayland (1)
and manages its windows like any other: the same frame, snapping, picker
and Task View. \fBDISPLAY\fR is set for programs the session starts. The
clipboard is shared in both directions. X11 applications can see each
other's windows and input, as on any X server; they cannot see Wayland
applications. X11 windows are drawn at scale 1 and enlarged on a scaled
monitor.
.SH SECURITY
Ordinary clients get the application protocols only: they cannot read the
screen, other windows or global key strokes.
.PP
The privileged protocols (layer shell for panels and overlays, session
lock, the window list, clipboard control, input method and virtual
keyboard) are offered only to programs the compositor started itself, over
a connection it created for them. Clients that connect through a security
context (Flatpak and other sandboxes) never get them.
\fBprivileged_protocols = "all"\fR offers them to every client outside a
sandbox; it exists for development and must not be set on a managed device.
.PP
Nobody sees the screen without the user's say. The Print key and the
\fBcapture\fR command open the capture screen of the shell, where the user
chooses what to keep and confirms. A program that asks for pictures of the
screen itself (a screenshot tool, a screen recorder, the desktop portal
behind screen sharing in browsers and meetings; the wlr\-screencopy
protocol) waits until the shell has asked "Allow \fIprogram\fR to see your
screen?" and the user has allowed it. The permission covers that program
while it keeps capturing and ends a few seconds after its last picture; a
mark on the taskbar shows while any program may capture, and a click on it
stops them all. A refusal holds for twenty seconds. Sandboxed programs are
not offered the protocol, and nothing is captured while the session is
locked.
.PP
The compositor owns the session lock. Locking blanks every output and takes
input away from applications before the lock screen is started; only a lock
client's unlock request ends it. If the lock screen exits without
unlocking, the outputs stay blank and it is started again. The session is
also locked before the system suspends or hibernates
(\fI/usr/lib/systemd/system\-sleep/boswas\-lock\fR).
.PP
The compositor runs as the logged-in user. Device access comes from the
login session (libseat, logind); it has no setuid bit and needs no
capability.
.SH CONTROL SOCKET
\fB$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/boswas\-\fIWAYLAND_DISPLAY\fB.sock\fR accepts one JSON
object per line, for example {"cmd": "outputs"}. Connections to this socket
may ask for \fBversion\fR, \fBlock\fR, \fBreload\-config\fR, \fBoutputs\fR
(every monitor with its modes, scale, position and rotation) and
\fBcapture\fR (opens the capture screen; "mode": "screen" or "region").
The window list, window control, desktops, thumbnails, screenshots, the
answer to a program that asks to see the screen and \fBquit\fR are
available to the shell only, on the descriptor the compositor hands it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BOSWAS_LOG
Log filter (for example \fBdebug\fR or \fBboswas_compositor=trace\fR).
Messages go to standard error.
.TP
.B XCURSOR_THEME
Cursor theme when \fBpointer.cursor_theme\fR is empty.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the session ended normally, 1 when the compositor could not start or
failed, 2 for a usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR boswas-session (1),
